# Node037
{1280}{}{It's often said that the road to Hell is paved with good intentions. Used to think it was just a cynical saying, but lately, it's been ringing truer than ever. Back in my days with the NCR in Shady Sands, I lived a respectable life as a councilman. Had dreams of climbing higher, maybe even becoming a senator. But alas, I never quite mastered the art of networking in those elite circles.}
{1281}{}{Yeah, it's like you can do everything right and still end up overlooked.}
{1282}{}{I've heard enough. Thanks for sharing.}

# Node038
{1310}{}{I always considered myself a man of action, not one to cozy up to others for advancement. Unfortunately, my "man-of-action" approach wasn't yielding results. I never seemed to encounter any real action. Sure, one must volunteer for such endeavors, which I failed to do, but in my mind, that was beside the point. So, I resigned myself to the role of a wallflower during my tenure as councilman - until volunteers were needed for an expedition eastward.}
{1311}{}{Please continue.}

# Node039
{1340}{}{Given the tense situation with the Brotherhood of Steel, the NCR couldn't risk sending their top commanders to pioneer the eastern lands. Instead, they tapped "expendable" officers to lead caravans in establishing outposts under the NCR banner. I, Councilman Joseph Dodge, found myself among these "fortunate" few. Initially, I tried to evade this "honor," but when refusal equated to treason, I had no choice but to accept.}
{1341}{}{A difficult position to be in.}

# Node040
{1370}{}{Thankfully, my mission wasn't a blind conquest. I was directed towards Hoover Dam with a clear objective. Before the war, the dam held immense power, both literal and technological. NCR coveted that power, especially amid rising tensions with the Brotherhood. The tech at Hoover Dam rivaled, if not surpassed, the Brotherhood's arsenal - a prize too tempting to ignore.}
{1371}{}{A strategic move by the NCR.}

# Node041
{1400}{}{Upon arrival, my caravans found struggling settlers fending off constant attacks from raiders. It didn't take much persuasion to convince them of the benefits of NCR protection and organization. Hoover Dam became the focal point, with our presence ensuring safety and stability for the region's inhabitants.}
{1401}{}{Logical course of action.}
{1402}{}{Look at the time fly by, Governor. I have to go now. Goodbye.}

# Node042
{1430}{}{With the settlers' enthusiastic cooperation, we fortified the dam's perimeter, instilling a sense of security among them. Swift action against raiders bolstered their confidence in our leadership.}
{1431}{}{You certainly made an impact.}

# Node043
{1460}{}{As the Hoover Dam community flourished, it began to develop its own social structure. The Dam became divided into two distinct areas: the Rim, where government officials and affluent businesspersons resided, and Downtown, home to the hardworking construction laborers and farmers. Naturally, my NCR troops and I settled in the Rim, where I earned the title of Governor among the people of Hoover Dam. I was honored as a just and upright leader who ushered the settlers into an era of enlightenment and self-worth.}
{1461}{}{An intriguing development.}

# Node044
{1490}{}{Trouble stirred when Caesar's Legion attempted to invade Hoover Dam. The conflict was fierce, and to this day, I'm perplexed by their motives. Nonetheless, with the combined efforts of myself, the NCR troops, and the formidable defense systems, including high-powered gun turrets, we thwarted Caesar's invasion plans.}
{1491}{}{The Legion stood no chance.}

# Node045
{1520}{}{The battles left the eastern Rim in ruins, rendering it uninhabitable. Debris piled up at the center, blocking passage to the east side. Despite this setback, our victory over Caesar's Legion solidified the security of both the Rim and Downtown. However, peace was short-lived as the conflict between the NCR and the BOS erupted.}
{1521}{}{War often leaves scars that linger.}

# Node046
{1550}{}{Initially, the Brotherhood's assaults were repelled thanks to our sturdy iron gates and powerful turrets. However, as months passed, our supply routes from the NCR base dwindled. Ammunition became scarce, and I feared our defenses would crumble against the Brotherhood's advanced weaponry. It was clear we needed an alternative source of arms to stand a chance.}
{1551}{}{What did you do?}
{1552}{}{Farewell for now.}

# Node047
{1580}{}{The Crimson Caravan Company, integral to Hoover Dam's growth, stepped up to the challenge. Led by Ailis McLafferty, they ventured out in search of a new weapons and supplies route. I entrusted them with this vital task, yet...}
{1581}{}{Yet what?}

# Node048
{1610}{}{Yet, this pact with the Crimson Caravan angered their rivals, the 3-Some Caravan Company. However, their objections didn't sway me. I had reservations about the 3-Some Caravan, being led by former NCR prisoners. I couldn't risk involving them in securing weapons outside of NCR supply runs.}

# Node049
{1640}{}{Nearly six months had passed, and my men were at their breaking point. Just as we were about to exhaust our ammunition, Ailis McLafferty arrived at the main gates with a truckload of finely crafted firearms and ammunition. She claimed to have struck a lucrative trade deal with a distant tribal community known for their mechanical prowess. However, she remained tight-lipped about the tribe's location and nature.}
{1641}{}{You don't say... a gunpowder-worshipping tribe?}

# Node050
{1670}{}{With the new weapons and ammunition, we were well-equipped to repel future Brotherhood assaults. I relished the notion that the Brotherhood might be feeling the strain of prolonged conflict, their supply lines dwindling just as ours had. Perhaps, I mused, the time would come for us to seize the initiative and deal a decisive blow to the weakened Brotherhood.}
{1671}{}{Yet fate had other plans?}

# Node051
{1700}{}{Unfortunately, the Brotherhood renewed their onslaught on Hoover Dam soon after our resupply. It seemed that as our arsenal grew, so did theirs. The notion that they might be faring better in the war than anticipated crossed my mind. Regardless, it became evident that we were locked in a stalemate, a war of attrition with no clear victor in sight. As a former councilman, I recognized the futility of such a protracted conflict.}
{1701}{}{A bleak realization indeed.}
{1702}{}{Thank you for sharing your story, Governor. Farewell for now.}

# Node052
{1730}{}{With NCR's shipments ceasing entirely, we became increasingly reliant on the resources brought in by our internal caravan companies. It became clear that in order to sustain our trading relations with external communities, we needed to tap into our own resources.}
{1731}{}{Shady Sands fell silent indeed.}

# Node053
{1760}{}{For a time, I utilized a construction site that was along the center of the Rim to gather materials for both building and trading. The abundance of resources such as rebar, stone, and steel beams proved invaluable. The demand for such materials was high, so trading these resources became profitable for both the Crimson Caravan and the 3-Some Caravan companies. Yet...}

# Node054
{1790}{}{Yet, it became evident that continued exploitation of the construction site would jeopardize the structural integrity of the dam itself. I ordered all access to the site be restricted under penalty of death.}
{1791}{}{A consequence of your self-reliance.}

# Node055
{1820}{}{Once again, we found ourselves in dire straits. The Crimson Caravan's mysterious trading partner refused to engage with us without valuable trade goods. Desperate for a solution, fortune smiled upon us when a ghoul resident named Otto brought forth intriguing information.}
{1821}{}{What did Otto reveal?}

# Node056
{1850}{}{Otto, as it turns out, oversees the water purification system in Downtown. While the water isn't fit for drinking, it's perfect for agriculture. What's fascinating is that the filtration process yields potassium nitrate and sulfur - key ingredients of black powder.}
{1851}{}{Fascinating indeed.}

# Node057
{1880}{}{Additionally, nitric acid, a vital gunpowder component, is also produced, albeit in smaller quantities. These chemicals are invaluable, especially to a community with access to coal. Fortunately, the community the Crimson Caravan deals with for arms and ammunition has a coal source but lacks a reliable supply of potassium nitrate and sulfur. Thus, trade resumed, cementing the Crimson Caravan's position as the top dog with me.}
{1881}{}{So, a mutually beneficial arrangement.}

# Node058
{1910}{}{With the weapons and ammunition issue resolved, another pressing concern emerged: battle fatigue. Despite reduced Brotherhood attacks, the constant state of alert took its toll on both soldiers and civilians. Some deserted Hoover Dam in search of respite from the relentless war.}
{1911}{}{They deserted?}

# Node059
{1940}{}{Those who remained felt a growing sense of apathy, leading to system malfunctions and breakdowns. The alcohol and drug abuse are on the rise. Otto, adept at mechanical work, struggles to keep up. I realize that unless the war ends, Hoover Dam faces certain doom - whether by the Brotherhood's hands or through internal collapse.}
{1941}{}{That's quite a story, Governor. Thanks for sharing but I really have to go now.}
{1942}{}{Is there anything I can do to assist you in ending the conflict?}

# Node060
{1970}{}{War with the Brotherhood has worn us all down. Though it's only been a few years, the toll is immense. Supplies dwindle, equipment fails, and tension remains high. I find myself torn between desperation and acceptance.}
{1971}{}{A challenging position to be in.}

# Node061
{2000}{}{I'm desperate for an end to the conflict, yet I accept the grim reality of our situation. Despite attempts at diplomacy, communication with the Brotherhood has proven fruitless. We've endured relentless attacks, and while they've subsided, I fear it's only a lull before a renewed onslaught. Their base lies to the East, but its exact location eludes us. You must convince the former Scribes in the Engineering Facility of our desire for peace. But keep this from Fleming. It could escalate matters beyond control.}
{2001}{}{Understood. I'll pursue peace or perish trying.}
{2002}{}{I wish you luck, Governor. Farewell for now.}

